title wholesale rename: a pool whose every name changes at once

# Built to test emission pairing; it tests REALIZATION instead, and
# that is why it is kept.
#
# Off-of renames every name of three pools. Onto is identical to
# base, so nothing conflicts. The decision materializes all three
# rather than reusing their onto dnodes, and the chain is coherent:
# a move of two names to two names has no unambiguous successor, so
# succession records nothing; with no substitution each output name
# looks itself up and finds nothing in base or onto; with no
# contributors, Definition 9.7 leaves the pool with no onto
# contributor, and Section 12 says such a pool is created fresh.
#
# The CONSEQUENCE is a question for the theory rather than a bug in
# either implementation: a hard-linked pair that one side renamed
# wholesale comes out on a brand-new dnode while its old dnode is
# deleted. On a real filesystem "mv x aa; mv y bb" preserves the
# inode. A plan that preserved it exists -- link the new names off
# an old one, then unlink the old ones, which never drops the link
# count to zero -- so identity is lost by the definition of
# contributors rather than by necessity.
#
# Pinned here so the behaviour is recorded whichever way the theory
# settles it.

tree base
  1-1: /
    5-100: x = xv0
    5-100: y
    6-100: m = mv0
    6-100: n
    7-100: p = pv0
    7-100: q
    7-100: r

tree onto
  1-1: /
    5-100: x = xv0
    5-100: y
    6-100: m = mv0
    6-100: n
    7-100: p = pv0
    7-100: q
    7-100: r

tree offof
  1-1: /
    5-100: aa = xv0
    5-100: bb
    6-100: cc = mv0
    6-100: dd
    6-100: ee
    7-100: ff = pv0
    7-100: gg
